      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
During the blower door test, we identified that your house has some air leakage associated with the following locations:

** The current 6" chimney for the furnace and domestic hot water, 6" chimney for the wood stove and 5"&amp; 4" combustion air supply pipes for the furnace and wood stove respectively. By replacing your current chimney vented furnace with a higher efficient direct vent condensing furnace and connecting a combustion air inlet pipe for the wood stove, you can eliminate the need for both the chimney pipes, as well as the 5"&amp; 4" combustion air supply pipes.
** The exterior doors - these doors are not sealing properly (can seal with weatherstripping).

Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 280 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 7 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <HotWaterSystem>
You currently have a 121 liter oil-fired hot water tank.You may want to consider replacing your oil-fired hot water heater with an oil-fired instantaneous (on-demand) water heater with an energy factor of 0.71.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water heater with an oil-fired instantaneous (on-demand) water heater with an energy factor of 0.71, you could potentially save $ 70 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with an oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 81%. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat and installing a dedicated combustion air inlet for the wood stove.

Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing oil furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower and connecting your current dedicated combustion air inlet for the wood stove, you could potentially save $ 1,550 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 39 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $3,500 is available for replacing an oil-fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR certified oil-fired furnace. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Wood Stove:

I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of oil saved from a new high efficiency furnace and the cost of wood in Tulita at the $100/cord "self-harvested" rate) if you use your current wood stove, used for about 50% of the heating requirement for the main floor of your house. Getting that wood heat throughout the main floor is something your heating contractor can work on by using the new furnace fan to circulate the wood heat throughout the house. I modeled an upgrade that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe.

Savings: By using your current 81% efficient wood stove for 50% of the heating requirements of your house, you could potentially save $ 2,550 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 95% AFUE oil-fired furnace with the ECM blower motor. 

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.

Savings: By installing a 4 kW solar photovoltaic system on the right facing roof of your house (orientation = SW), you could potentially save $ 1,000 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount is based on the lower of:
1. 50% of the eligible cost of the system, up to a maximum of $20,000.
2. A calculation of $4.00 per watt that the system can produce.

Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Photovoltaic Solar Panels: Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,300 per kW DC is available for installing a solar photovoltaic (PV) system. If you choose to install a PV system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar PV installer, including the measurement of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. 

The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW DC. The system must be composed of PV panels and an inverter certified to CSA Standards. Solar panels can be mounted on the house or ground, as long as they are on the property / land of the house associated with the application. Building permits may be required for both off-grid and grid-connected installations. For more information, refer to Natural Resources Canadas Solar Ready Guidelines document that can be found online.</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:
- In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
- If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
- Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
- All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
- It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
--- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
--- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
--- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
- All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.
Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights  Non-hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Tulita, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the rangehood fan, clothes dryer and bathroom fans), indicated a pressure drop of 2.1 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace, domestic hot water tank and wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting. 

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 
               - a clothes dryer
               - two bathroom fans
               - a rangehood fan

You may want to consider installing a bathroom exhaust fan with ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs  Tulita

The following Dec. 1, 2022 values, excluding GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Tulita:

Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to subsidy limit, after subsidy $0.69 per kWh
Oil - $1.47 per liter 
Wood - $608 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

** Oil &amp; Biomass fuels in Tulita tend to experience cyclical fuel price volatility. The Arctic Energy Alliance therefore uses 5-year rolling average for these values to better help you evaluate longer term heating appliance purchase/upgrade decisions.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Abdul Mohammed, Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
Phone: (867) 920-3333</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
